"Zmann" <zmaniac@gmail.com> wrote:

> So, the lack of replies tells me one of three
> things.

> a) It's impossible.

Software source code is a fungible good, where the
"freely" has to be interpreted to mean "modulo a
slogging lot of very hard work". Nothing about
modifying it is directly impossible beyond the
Halting Problem Solver kind of impossibility.

> b) No one knows.

Or the one who knew has died, or gone on to life in
the real world, or is too busy earning a living to
worry about your tiny inconveniences. This project
has been under continuous maintenance for two
decades, perfect continuity is a flat impossibility
in a software project that long lasting.

> c) No one cares.

Probably c. The current icons are something the rest
of us have found we can endure in preference to the
effort of figuring out how to change them.

Your question has been raised at least once and
discussed in the previous two or three years of my
most recent return to RGRN, an archives search might
reveal that someone once provide a useful response.

> All of the possib[i]lities include me not getting
> the answer, so thanks for your help.

Sarcasm isn't too useful an approach to getting help
in a group where dying often is a way of life, and
where software maintenance is an example of
functioning anarchy and done entirely by volunteers
whose good opinions you need to keep to expect any
help _ever_.

I'm pretty sure the icons used in the menus are
completely independent of the icons used on the map,
since changing the map icons to the absurd tile set
didn't modify the menu ones for me at all.

But, I'm willing to confess having been wrong a time
or two in the past, that may be the case here as
well, since the smaller icons are compiled straight
into the code, best I can tell, while the large ones
are accessed as external data instead; they might
well coexist at run time.

It took me many days to thread through the creation
mechanism for the NetHack tiles to the point that I
felt comfortable with repairing some of the large
ones, and in an ideal world, I'd fix up some set of
large ones to fit into an imitation of the obscenely
complex mechanism by which the normal ones are
maintained, and add software to put the large tile
creation into the NetHack code, but this isn't that
world; in this world, I'm old, tired, lazy, and
insane.

Even finding stuff in the NetHack build is hideously
complex, in part because the NetHack build recurses
on itself. It does a build to create executables,
which are then run still in the build stream to
create source code, which is then compiled by
another pass in the build stream to create the game
executables, and things may be worse even than that,
that's just the part I traced through recently in
search of some one factoid.

All of this applied to old, barely commented,
deviantly twisted code written "cleverly" [a synonym
for both "unreadably" and "unmaintainably"] (gag) in
a purely imperative coding technology most modern
programming has abandoned for OOP or functional
programming or both.

Then there's the extra programming languages hidden
in NetHack, like the level builder...

Getting the dependencies correct in a makefile that
does that messy, messy build has to have been a
labor of love. Analyzing it, or worse yet
maintaining it, is an act of purest masochism.

So, essentially, anytime you want to figure out
something about NetHack's graphics (whose level
tiles format is _circular_; the three forms can be
converted one to the next in a _ring_), your only
resource is very likely to be yourself.

Lots of luck drilling through the layers of code,
and write if you hit oil.

FWIW

xanthian.

